# Build context for Containerfile.
#
# The build needs exactly: Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock, rust-toolchain.toml, src/,
# app/, vendor/rustex1/ (the kit + devserver crates the build depends on),
# web/ (index.html, styles.css — pkg/ is generated), build/ (the staged binary,
# the vendored wasm-bindgen tarballs, and the optional trust anchors in
# build/certs/), deploy/ (the entrypoint). Everything here is either residue or a
# reference.

# Build output — a host `cargo build` leaves a multi-GB target/ here, and
# shipping it would both bloat the context and hand the build a stale tree.
target/
**/target/
wasm32-unknown-unknown/

# The dev-time wasm-bindgen CLI dev.sh fetches into tools/. The Containerfile
# downloads its own, pinned to the version vendor/rustex1/kit/Cargo.toml names.
tools/

# Not build input for a Rust → wasm app.
node_modules/

# Working notes: plans, benchmarks, scratch. No build or runtime reads them.
tmp/
docs/

# Local deployment state — the CD/manifest trees this repo is deployed BY, not
together with. None of it is build input, and `COPY . .` in the build stage would
otherwise carry an entire environment's hostnames, cluster names and secret
references into a layer. Gitignore alone does not protect you: podman reads this
file, not .gitignore, and the two lists are allowed to differ.
.*-local/
.deploy/
k8s/

# Git metadata: 20 MB of history the image has no use for, and the entrypoint
# clones the *data* repo at run time rather than relying on this one's.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitmodules
.containerignore

# Local agent/tooling config that must not reach a build artifact.
.env
.envrc
.keys.yaml
.tokens.yaml

# NOTE: *.crt / *.pem are deliberately NOT excluded here, even though .gitignore
# ignores them. build/certs/*.crt is a legitimate build input, and this file — not
# .gitignore — is what podman reads when it decides what reaches the daemon.
# Ignoring certificates here would make the trust-anchor feature unbuildable.
